Solving for $\cos(\theta_1)$, we get the following:

$\cos(\theta_1)=\pm\sqrt{1-\sin^2(\theta_1)}$

Plugging $\sin(\theta_1)=\dfrac{11}{61}$, we get the following:

$\begin{align}
\cos(\theta_1)&=\pm\sqrt{\dfrac{3600}{3721}}
\\\\
&=\pm\dfrac{60}{61}
\end{align}$

To figure out the sign of $\cos(\theta_1)$, we should use the fact that $\theta_1$ is located in Quadrant $\text{I}$.
